  "worked_solution": "We wish to express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{3})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle corresponding to the angle $\\theta =-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nFirst, we find the corresponding coterminal angle that lies in the range  $[0,2\\pi ).$ We do this by adding integer multiples of $2\\pi $ until we get our desired angle:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+2\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,✓\n$$\n\nNotice that our coterminal angle is acute and lies in the first quadrant. Therefore, ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\n \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266661/assets/dcd85f0a3f6a7affdfbc58f4ff52b1c46b44cf2a55282bf0497fc822107883d4.png)",
